00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.230 align:start position:0%
 
uh labour's shadow leveling up secretary  and mp for wigan lisa nandy is with us  hi hello good morning thanks for  allowing us to come to this amazing time  um  coverage this week and just chatting to  people  it's been amazing it's brilliant the  whole town is buzzing because you've  been everywhere this week i've been  following your progress beach hill  primary school the  brick all over the town and seeing the  amazing stuff that people are doing here  even in really difficult times even with  the government that is standing aside  while people are in crisis we're  stepping up and getting on with it  that's what as you know this town has  always done people are doing it right  across the country but this week in  parliament the government had a chance  to actually back their people didn't do  it and i've just got back from london  just got home and i'm pretty cross about  it  well you say that but you know the  government would say in response to that  we are heading towards a recession  they're doing absolutely everything they  can it looks as though we will see a tax  decrease probably in the summer there  isn't a magic money tree they can only  do so much  some of the measures that we proposed  yesterday in parliament that the  government voted down don't actually  cost the public any money we called for  a windfall tax on the oil and gas  producers they made record profits it's  only 600 quid a household even if it  goes through but you know that every  single pound that goes into people's  pockets at the moment can be the  difference between make or break most  people now are struggling to keep their  heads above water we also ask them to to  cancel their planned national insurance  contributions rise  we had a lot of money coming into the  into the coffers we are the only  developed country that's putting up  taxes on working people in the middle of  not just an inflation crisis but also  coming out the other side of a global  pandemic and you've heard it all week  you've heard it from people in every  part of this town not just people who  were struggling before but people who've  never struggled before and are now just  finding that they don't know how they're  going to pay their rent their mortgage  they don't know how they're going to  afford their weekly shop as well as  their energy bill and the government  should be doing every single thing that  they can moving heaven and earth to try  and help people at the moment well the  national insurance increase is bringing  in a lot of money so they would say of  course that's what we need to do at the  moment a windfall tax would only bring  in about two billion pounds it's just a  drop in the bucket so what would labour  do well as as well as those two things  the other thing that we proposed  yesterday was cracking on with  retrofitting homes putting home  insulation into people's homes well  that's been an ongoing program that has  fallen off a cliff under this government  but the councils are raring to go to to  to do it's an investment k this is the  point what was it  but it would it would cut 400 pounds of  people's energy bills and you know that  if you get every year and if you get  money back into working people's pockets  they go out and spend it when you walk  up and down the high street you must see  that although we're still doing okay  here in wigan you've got money lenders  boarded up shops at the bottom of the  high street we didn't have those 10  years ago and that's because we  prioritized putting money into people's  pockets so they could go out and spend  it it's nonsense to talk about leveling  up this country as the government used  to do but seems to have abandoned when  you're not putting money into working  people's pockets where's the investment  going to come from to to make people's  homes less drafty et cetera where's that  money coming from well look it's an  investment  first few measures that we proposed are  cost neutral to the public purse the  third one  is about potentially bringing forward  benefit operating so you calculate the  amount that benefits increase every year  based on inflation the current benefit  system is based on in the inflation rate  last year which was dramatically lower  so one thing that the government could  do that wouldn't cost any money is bring  that forward and we asked them to look  at that as well yesterday every single  one of those measures the chancellor set  his face against and said not now but  now is the moment when this money and  this help really counts for people we  need a government that backs our people  we're doing great work as you can see  but we need a government that steps up  and helps us now you say that the  government is not in charge but some of  the people i've been talking to this  week have talked about that posh bloke  who's been knighted with a really nice  suit on who comes from a really posh  part of london you know how much is he  in touch with what's happening here i  think he's very in touch he and i are  going to be in leeds in just a few hours  time talking to people about the cost of  living crisis we've been all over the  country to grimsby to burnley to  doncaster in recent months listening to  what people have got to say which is why  we came forward with this package of  emergency measures in the budget this  week in the in the queen's speech this  week to try to get the government to  bring forward an emergency budget i  think people do appreciate actually that  kierstama is trying to make sure that  the main concern for most people is  heard and dealt with at the highest  levels of government if he were prime  minister right now people would not be  struggling to survive and put food on  the table because we would be knocking  600 pounds off energy bills for those  who most need it 200 pounds for  everybody and bringing forward an  emergency budget to actually start to  tackle this problem what is it about the  posh borough of islington and labour  leaders though you know we've had four  or five only one of them has been  successful  well look throughout our history labour  has had to fight really hard to gain the  public's trust and inspire people to  vote for us we've only won three times  in our entire history i'm determining  that the next time is going to be at the  next election and i'll tell you why  because i wake up every single day in my  town looking around me thinking imagine  what we could do with the government  that backed us we've survived really  tough times here in wigan you know that  when the mines closed it was really  difficult a decade of austerity has not  been kind to us wigan council has had  the third worst budget cuts in the  country but despite all of that look at  the spirit here look at what we're doing  and imagine what we could do with the  government that backed us take questions  before i let you go i know you need to  go to lee's the sun's saying this  morning that labour might not might get  as many as 20 police questionnaires over  beer gates it's just bad as downstream  well look we've already been cleared for  this once and i'm confident that we'll  be cleared again i'm not surprised that  the tories are trying to throw mud at us  i think it suits them to talk about that  when they don't want to talk about  what's actually happening to people in  places like wigan and we're determined  that that's not going to happen right up  and down this country at the moment  people are in crisis people are in pain  and we're going to do something to sort  that out  why do you think the toys have not  withdrawn the work from an mp that's  been accused of rape i don't know but i  share the view of many conservative  female mps who've spoken out in the last  few hours about that over the last 24  hours since this news came into the  public domain i just think this is  disgraceful parliament is supposed to be  not just a safe place to work not just  for mps but for all of the staff who  work there but also we're supposed to  set an example to the rest of the  country and i just genuinely cannot  understand that decision i want to give  those brave conservative mps who've  spoken out about the decision by their  own wits my absolute full backing today  something has got to be done 50 odd mps  including from your own front bench  being investigated i mean is it not a  safe space for women in the chamber i  mean look i don't think parliament is a  particularly modern workplace i would  love us to move out of the building it's  sinking into the mud it's riddled with  asbestos it needs serious work i'd love  us to turn that building into a place  that people could come and look around a  museum something to learn about the  history of parliamentary democracy and  go and work in a modern workplace where  people have normal decent professional  standards most people who work there are  decent they are professional but there's  still far too much of an old boys club  about it it means that people like me  when you walk in there for the first  time  is disgraceful but i also think it's  cultural i think there's some kind of  feeling of entitlement amongst some of  the members of parliament not not many i  have to say most of us are quite normal  people but there is this feeling of  arrogance of entitlement this culture  that has grown up that i think we've got  to  consign to the bin of history this is  2022 and we cannot continue with a  situation where a small minority of  people are able to go and walk around  behaving like this at the heart of our  democracy i must let you please it's  good to talk to you thanks very much

